## LONG

**1. `lnbrookngs-aurorawa` — highest conviction. Rung 1 (outage), reinforced by rung 3.**
Linked outage **Split Rock – White 345 kV Ckt 1, 2026-07-29 07:00 → 2026-08-20 16:00 — active on the bid date.** The PDF (analysis 08-06) names exactly this: *"the actual scheduled outage of that same White to Split Rock 345 kV line… removed a major parallel path and dumped approximately 6.8 MW of flow onto the 115 kV monitored element."* RT bound **-7,662 yesterday** (2nd largest on the board) and **DA has been 0 every single day of the last 7**. 7/30 RT days. Driver: wind (WAUE/NSP structural transfer), zone 4 fallback so the lift is low-confidence — prefer the PDF's WAUE/NSP naming. This is the largest DA/RT disagreement available.

**2. `lnvinetap3-nhays` — rung 1 (fresh outage) + this morning's RT.**
Contingency is `midw:knoll1xfa:230115:1:6`; scheduled outage **SALINE – KNOLL1 115 kV, 2026-08-14 06:00 → 2026-08-18 17:00** is active tomorrow — same station, adjacent element, not the identical breaker, so treat it as strong circumstantial rather than a topology identity. Timing lines up: the outage started 08-14, and RT this morning is **-872 against a DA of -37**, a 24× gap. 11/30 RT days; also -7,983 RT on 08-11. **Caveat stated plainly:** the PDF (08-03) attributes this to *"Peak Wind… avg 19,868 MW"* in WR/MIDW/OKGE — tomorrow zone 2 wind is at the **1st–4th percentile**. The wind story does **not** reproduce. This call rests on the outage and this morning's RT, not on the write-up. Only candidate with a seasonal precedent (6 RT / 12 DA days in the same window last year).

**3. `xfmrbrook_ln-brook_ln` — rung 3, cleanest DA blind spot.**
**25/30 RT days** — second-highest frequency on the board — and **DA has printed exactly 0 on all 7 of the last 7 days.** RT: -2,364 / -181 / -250 / -1,254 / -5,684 / -1,877. PDF (08-04) attributes it to SPRM+EES peak load with *"no transmission outages found to explain the real-time divergence"* — so this is structural, not outage-contingent, which is why the expiry check doesn't hurt it. Load-driven (lift 4.07 vs wind -0.36), but **zone 4 fallback — low confidence, measured against the wrong zone's forecast; the PDF names SPRM.** Zone 4 load tomorrow is only mid (51/59), so size it on the frequency, not the conditions.

**4. `xfmrnowst1-nowst1` — rung 3/4, largest absolute RT on the board.**
21/30 RT days; RT -11k, -9.2k, -9.3k, -15.2k, **-27.0k**, **-23.6k** across 08-09→08-14 against a DA that was 0 on four of those six days. Load-driven, zone 4, lift **10.74** (wind 1.51). **Condition caveat:** the PDF (08-05) requires *"extreme peak load in the OKGE zone… 6,156 MW avg"*; tomorrow zone 4 peaks at 33,774 MW / **51.6 pct** — mild by comparison, and today's DA has already jumped to -6,694. So expect it to bind, but the DA/RT gap should be far narrower than last week's. Long, sized down.

**5. `lnrussett-sbrown` — rung 3/4, most frequent binder.**
**29/30 RT days.** RT **-3,111 in the first 8 hours today** against a DA of -1,512; -3,796 yesterday. **Both PDF outages have expired** — PITTSB9 2160 ended 08-07 17:00, CRIVRAEP XF1 ended 08-14 16:00 — and it is binding hard anyway, so I am explicitly **not** resting this on the write-up. Zone 3, wind-driven (lift 1.07, load -0.43); zone 3 wind is best **overnight (29–39 pct HE1–5)** and collapses to 6.5 pct by evening, so weight the overnight block.

**6. `lnosage_og-webbtap4` — rung 1, but with the acute trigger missing.**
**Morrison – Sooner 138 kV, 2026-07-23 08:00 → 2026-09-17 15:30 — active tomorrow**, confirmed in `sched_outages`. PDF (08-03): the outage *"served as a critical predisposition, increasing the odds of binding by 7.17x."* 18/30 RT days, RT -708 this morning ≈ DA -719. But the PDF's acute trigger was *"peak wind generation in the OKGE zone"* and tomorrow's zone 3 wind is 24/35 median, 6.5 pct by evening — **the predisposition holds, the trigger does not.** Moderate long, overnight-weighted.

**7. `lnmaud_tap-earlsbrh` — rung 3, RT persistence against a collapsing DA.**
RT bound every day 08-09→08-14 (-2,205 / -1,852 / -4,295 / **-10,312** / **-10,789** / -1,943), 15/30 days. Today's DA has dropped to **-498** from -6,048 on 08-13. **The PDF's trigger — the Seminole–Pittsburg 345 kV outage — expired 08-07**, yet RT kept binding for a week afterwards, so the write-up understates it. Load-driven (lift 7.13, wind 1.97), zone 4 fallback so low confidence. Long on the RT-vs-deflating-DA gap.

**8. `lnmdwst-frnkln1` — rung 3.**
RT **-3,720 yesterday**, 15/30 RT days, DA today only -323. Load lift **14.04**, wind -1.00, zone 4 fallback (low confidence). PDF (08-01) mechanism is unusual and worth noting: *"OKGE zonal load coming in significantly lower than forecast… Because OKGE load relieves this constraint, the lower-than-expected load removed critical relief."* Tomorrow's mild zone 4 load is therefore **aggravating**, not relieving — the one candidate where mild load argues *for* the long.

**9. `lnfulton_c-pat_wx` and `lnmanfl7-clec_pel` — rung 3, DA blind spots.**
Both have **DA = 0 on all 7 of the last 7 days**. `lnfulton_c-pat_wx` bound RT every day this week (11/30 days, -1,724 on 08-13); `lnmanfl7-clec_pel` 13/30 days (-2,436 yesterday). Neither is in the PDF. Both zone 4 fallback — low confidence, load-tilted lift. Smaller, but the DA/RT disagreement is systematic.

**10. `lnhoyt-54mr` — low conviction, rung 3 only.**
2/30 RT days, but bound RT **yesterday (-488) and again this morning (-240)** with DA at 0 all week. Interesting because the PDF (08-05) describes the *opposite* regime — bound DA, cleared RT on WR load over-forecast. Lift is nil (load -0.31, wind -1.00) and there's **no precedent in the same window last year**. Small speculative long; flagging it rather than sizing it.

